### What happens

`rotationDegreesZ` on `ModifyMorphs` is described in the Grasshopper component as "Rotation around the vertical axis in degrees", and the name says Z. The add-on instead recombines rows 0 and 2 of the morph's `tranmat`, which is a rotation about the world **Y** axis. Because the loop also covers column 3 (the translation), the morph is swung around the project origin as well.

Reproduced in Archicad 29 (build 5101) with Tapir 1.7.1: a `CreateMorphs` box with `basePoint` (10, 0, 0) and `size` (1, 2, 0.5), then `ModifyMorphs` with `rotationDegreesZ: 90`:

| | Expected (turn about the vertical axis through the morph's origin) | Tapir 1.7.1 |
|---|---|---|
| Morph origin (`GetDetailsOfElements`) | (10, 0, 0) | (0, 0, -10) |
| 3D bounding box (`Get3DBoundingBoxes`) | x 8 to 10, y 0 to 1, z 0 to 0.5 | x 0 to 0.5, y 0 to 2, z -11 to -10 |
| Local +X points to | world +Y | world -Z (straight down) |

So the morph ends up 10 m below project zero, lying on its side.

### Where

`archicad-addon/Sources/ExtendedElementCommands.cpp`, `ModifyMorphsCommand::Execute`:

- main: https://github.com/ENZYME-APD/tapir-archicad-automation/blob/a4e4a745feb31eb5c4910937af59d529d9323885/archicad-addon/Sources/ExtendedElementCommands.cpp#L6832-L6845
- 1.7.1: https://github.com/ENZYME-APD/tapir-archicad-automation/blob/1.7.1/archicad-addon/Sources/ExtendedElementCommands.cpp#L6735-L6748

The same block has been there since 1.4.0 (#369):

```cpp
for (Int32 column = 0; column < 4; ++column) {
    element.morph.tranmat.tmx[column] = cosAngle * originalTransform.tmx[column] + sinAngle * originalTransform.tmx[8 + column];
    element.morph.tranmat.tmx[8 + column] = -sinAngle * originalTransform.tmx[column] + cosAngle * originalTransform.tmx[8 + column];
}
```

Open PR #463 moves this block unchanged into `ApplyMorphDetails`, so `SetDetailsOfElements` would get the same behaviour for morphs.

### Why

`APIdefs_Base.h` defines `API_Tranmat` as:

```
x' = tmx[0] * x + tmx[1] * y + tmx[2]  * z + tmx[3]
y' = tmx[4] * x + tmx[5] * y + tmx[6]  * z + tmx[7]
z' = tmx[8] * x + tmx[9] * y + tmx[10] * z + tmx[11]
```

Rows 0, 1 and 2 produce world x', y' and z'. Replacing row 0 with `cos * row0 + sin * row2` and row 2 with `-sin * row0 + cos * row2` is a left multiplication by the rotation about Y. A turn about the vertical axis has to recombine x' and y', that is rows 0 and 1.

Running the loop over column 3 also rotates the translation, which puts the pivot at the project origin. `ModifyMorphs` has no pivot input (unlike `RotateElements`, which takes an explicit `origin`), so turning the morph in place about its own origin looks like the intent. A side effect of the current code: `translation` is applied before the rotation in the same call, so a translation given together with `rotationDegreesZ` is rotated too.

### Suggested fix

Recombine rows 0 and 1, over the 3x3 part only, so the translation column stays put:

```cpp
if (rotationDegrees.HasValue ()) {
    const double radians = rotationDegrees.Get () * DegreesToRadians;
    const double cosAngle = std::cos (radians);
    const double sinAngle = std::sin (radians);
    const API_Tranmat originalTransform = element.morph.tranmat;
    // Turn about the vertical axis through the morph's origin: recombine rows 0 (x') and 1 (y')
    // of the 3x3 part only, so the translation (tmx[3], tmx[7], tmx[11]) is kept.
    for (Int32 column = 0; column < 3; ++column) {
        element.morph.tranmat.tmx[column] = cosAngle * originalTransform.tmx[column] - sinAngle * originalTransform.tmx[4 + column];
        element.morph.tranmat.tmx[4 + column] = sinAngle * originalTransform.tmx[column] + cosAngle * originalTransform.tmx[4 + column];
    }
    ACAPI_ELEMENT_MASK_SET (mask, API_MorphType, tranmat);
    changed = true;
}
```

I tried this in a local AC29 build of main with an example script (`modify_morph_rotation.py`) and all its checks pass in Archicad 29: at +90 the box spans x 8 to 10, y 0 to 1 and the origin stays at (10, 0, 0); a turn of +45 then -45 changes nothing; a `translation` of (1, 0, 0) given with -90 in one call moves the origin by exactly (1, 0, 0). Also checked numerically: a positive angle turns counterclockwise seen from above, like other Archicad angles, and a tilted morph keeps its tilt against the world. Happy to open a PR with the fix and the example.

This turns about the world vertical axis. If "about the morph's own Z axis" is preferred for tilted morphs, recombine columns 0 and 1 instead (right multiplication); for an untilted morph both give the same result.

It would also help to give the field a description in the schema, for example:

```json
"rotationDegreesZ": { "type": "number", "description": "Turns the Morph about the vertical axis through its origin, counterclockwise seen from above, in degrees. Added to the current rotation." }
```

One question for the maintainers: the pivot is the morph's placement origin. For morphs from `CreateMorphs` that is `basePoint`. I have not checked where Archicad puts the origin of a morph drawn by hand; if that is the project origin, those morphs would still turn about the project origin, and a pivot input (or the bounding box centre) might be more useful.

### Related, separate (not part of this fix)

`GetDetailsOfElements` reports a morph's `xAxis`, `yAxis` and `zAxis` as the **rows** of `tranmat` (`tmx[0..2]`, `tmx[4..6]`, `tmx[8..10]`, in `AddMorphBodyFromMemo`), and `CreateMorphs` / `ModifyMorphs` write them back as rows (`ApplyMorphCosmeticDetails`). By the definition above, the direction of the morph's local X in world coordinates is column 0 (`tmx[0]`, `tmx[4]`, `tmx[8]`). Reads and writes agree with each other, and rows and columns agree for an unrotated morph, but after the fixed +90 turn `xAxis` reads (0, -1, 0) in Archicad while the morph's local X points to +Y (its bounding box confirms it). Worth a schema note at least (the axes are matrix rows), or a separate look.

### What I'd like

`CreateAssociativeDimensions` only accepts witness points that reference an element: `elementId` is required and there is no way to give a position. Archicad itself also supports **static witness points** that are not attached to any element — the ones you get when you add a dimension point with the pet palette on empty space. I'd like `CreateAssociativeDimensions` to accept such points by coordinate:

```json
{
  "dimensionsData": [{
    "referencePoint": {"x": 66.0759, "y": 125.0275},
    "direction": {"x": 0, "y": -1},
    "witnessPoints": [
      {"elementId": {"guid": "68EB8255-2E38-AE54-DE0E-2BFBED9F581C"}, "line": true, "inIndex": 10},
      {"coordinate": {"x": 66.2544, "y": 55.9975}},
      {"elementId": {"guid": "D4BBECD7-2689-4C9B-B2CA-AA0A55E4E9C4"}, "line": true, "inIndex": 10}
    ]
  }]
}
```

A witness point would take either `elementId` (associative, as today) or `coordinate` (static), not both.

### Why

I use a script that, for each line/polyline selected on the floor plan, creates a dimension chain along it, dimensioning the outer faces of the walls, curtain walls and columns the line crosses. Straight walls crossed at right angles and rectangular columns work well with associative references (`line: true` + `inIndex` 10/20 for wall faces, the corner `inIndex` for columns). But some crossings have no element reference that measures the exact crossing point:

- curtain walls: references to the curtain wall, its frames or its panels give (0,0) or the dimension gets discarded;
- walls crossed at an angle: the face reference is anchored at the middle of the face, so the measured position is off;
- curved and polygonal walls.

Today the only workaround is to create a Hotspot at each crossing and attach the point to it. That leaves extra elements in the model, and deleting those hotspots also removes the points from the dimension chain (tested). A static point is the clean solution, and it is what a user does by hand.

### Evidence that Archicad supports it

A point added by hand with the pet palette is returned by `GetDimensionData` like this — no `baseElementId`, and `coordinate` is its position:

```json
{
  "coordinate": {"x": 66.254384812, "y": 55.997501466},
  "line": false,
  "inIndex": 0,
  "special": 0,
  "nodeType": 0,
  "nodeStatus": 0,
  "nodeId": 0
}
```

So the point has a zombie base (`API_ZombieElemID`, null GUID) and its position lives in `dimElem.base.loc`.

### Suggested change

When a witness point has `coordinate` instead of `elementId`, create it with a zombie base and set `dimElem.base.loc`. Static points are skipped when the floor is taken from the referenced elements (the `floorIndex` parameter or the default still applies). I haven't been able to build or test this (no DevKit setup here), so please treat it as a sketch:

```diff
--- a/archicad-addon/Sources/ExtendedElementCommands.cpp
+++ b/archicad-addon/Sources/ExtendedElementCommands.cpp
@@ struct AssociativeDimensionPoint {
     API_Guid elementGuid = APINULLGuid;
     API_ElemTypeID elementType = API_ZombieElemID;
+    bool isStatic = false;              // not associated with any element
+    API_Coord coordinate = {0.0, 0.0};  // position of a static point
     bool line = false;
@@ GS::Optional<GS::UniString> ParseAssociativeDimensionPoint (...)
     const GS::ObjectState* elementId = pointData.Get ("elementId");
+    const GS::ObjectState* coordinate = pointData.Get ("coordinate");
     if (elementId == nullptr) {
-        return "Missing required field 'elementId'.";
+        if (coordinate == nullptr) {
+            return "Each witness point needs either 'elementId' or 'coordinate'.";
+        }
+        // Static witness point: not associated with any element, placed at the given coordinate.
+        point.isStatic = true;
+        point.elementGuid = APINULLGuid;
+        point.elementType = API_ZombieElemID;
+        point.coordinate = Get2DCoordinateFromObjectState (*coordinate);
+        return {};
+    }
+    if (coordinate != nullptr) {
+        return "A witness point takes either 'elementId' or 'coordinate', not both.";
     }
@@ GS::Optional<GS::UniString> PopulateAssociativeDimensionMemo (...)
         dimElem.base.base.node_id = point.nodeId;
+        if (point.isStatic) {
+            dimElem.base.loc = point.coordinate;
+        }
         dimElem.note = element.dimension.defNote;
@@ void TryApplyDimensionFloorIndex (...)
     for (const AssociativeDimensionPoint& point : points) {
+        if (point.isStatic) {
+            continue;
+        }
         API_Elem_Head elementHeader = {};

--- a/archicad-addon/Sources/RFIX/Images/CommonSchemaDefinitions.json
+++ b/archicad-addon/Sources/RFIX/Images/CommonSchemaDefinitions.json
@@ "AssociativeWitnessPoint": {
         "type": "object",
+        "description": "A witness point of a dimension. Give elementId (and line, inIndex, ...) for a point associated with an element, or only coordinate for a static point that is not associated with any element.",
         "properties": {
             "elementId": { "$ref": "#/ElementId" },
+            "coordinate": {
+                "$ref": "#/Coordinate2D",
+                "description": "The position of a static (non-associative) witness point. Use it instead of elementId."
+            },
             ...
         },
-        "additionalProperties": false,
-        "required": [ "elementId" ]
+        "additionalProperties": false
     },
```

`GetDimensionData` already reports static points correctly (coordinate, no `baseElementId`), so nothing changes there.

