### Summary

Investigating real-world usage of `DeleteNavigatorItems` and `MoveNavigatorItem` against a large existing project (AC27, Mac), with two findings that look like real limitations, not obviously Tapir bugs - filing to check whether anything can be done about either, or whether they are genuine DevKit/Archicad restrictions worth documenting.

### 1. `DeleteNavigatorItems` refuses more than layout subsets

`DeleteNavigatorItemsCommand::Execute` (`NavigatorCommands.cpp`) does:

```cpp
if (navItem.itemType == API_LayoutNavItem || navItem.itemType == API_MasterLayoutNavItem) {
    ... ACAPI_Database_DeleteDatabase ...
} else if (navItem.itemType == API_SubSetNavItem) {
    err = APIERR_NOTSUPPORTED;
} else {
    bool silentMode = true;
    err = ACAPI_Navigator_DeleteNavigatorView (&guid, &silentMode);
}
```

The error message for `APIERR_NOTSUPPORTED` says "Deleting layout subsets is not supported by the ArchiCAD API." - but I added a temporary diagnostic printing `navItem.itemType` and confirmed this message also fires for:

- A View Map **folder** (`itemType == 24`, `API_FolderNavItem`), going through the `else` branch - `ACAPI_Navigator_DeleteNavigatorView` itself returns `APIERR_NOTSUPPORTED` for it, not the explicit `API_SubSetNavItem` check.
- A **TOC/schedule-index item** (`itemType == 9`, `API_TocNavItem`), same path, same result.

So the restriction isn't limited to layout subsets - at least View Map folders and TOC items hit it too, through `ACAPI_Navigator_DeleteNavigatorView` itself refusing them, not through the `API_SubSetNavItem` branch. Both items I tested were real, user-created items in a live project (a View Map folder created by `CreateViewMapFolder` earlier in the same session, and a pre-existing TOC/index schedule), not Archicad built-ins.

**Question**: is this a genuine Archicad API restriction (these item types simply cannot be deleted through `ACAPI_Navigator_DeleteNavigatorView`, full stop), or is there a different function/approach for deleting a View Map folder or a TOC item? If it's a hard restriction, the error message should probably stop blaming "layout subsets" specifically and say something accurate for whichever type actually triggered it.

### 2. Reordering items within the same parent via `MoveNavigatorItem` is unreliable

Using `MoveNavigatorItem` (`ACAPI_Navigator_SetNavigatorItemPosition` under the hood) to reorder ~170 View Map items to match a reference project's order (same parent, just a different `previousNavigatorItemId`):

- Story-based view items (leaves named after a story, e.g. `R+1`, `RDC`, `SS-1`, `---`) failed almost every time with `APIERR_BADPARS` (code `-2130313112`), both when their parent was also changing and when only reordering within an already-correct parent.
- Calls for plain View Map **folders** at the same parent (reordering 10 folders directly under the View Map root into a different order) returned `success: true` with no error, but a `GetNavigatorItemTree` fetched right after still showed the original order - no visible effect.

The DevKit doc for `ACAPI_Navigator_SetNavigatorItemPosition` does list `APIERR_BADPARS` as a possible outcome for "the source item cannot be moved to the desired destination", so the story-item failures may be an intentional restriction (story-based views ordered by story sequence, not freely reorderable) - that would be useful to have confirmed. The folder case (`success: true`, no actual change) is harder to explain the same way, and looks more like a real bug or a refresh/caching gap between the write and the next read.

**Question**: is View Map item order expected to be settable via `ACAPI_Navigator_SetNavigatorItemPosition` for plain folders, and is reordering story-based views specifically meant to be refused? Any clarification on the right way to reorder a View Map (if there is one) would help - today it looks like Tapir's `MoveNavigatorItem` can relocate items between folders reliably, but can't be used to purely reorder siblings.

### Context

Archicad 27 (Mac), Tapir built from `upstream/main` + a few unmerged PRs, live-tested against a real, large existing project (not a toy test file) while reorganizing its View Map to match a reference copy.

## Context

I'm trying to replicate a Layout Book's drawing placements from one ArchiCAD project to another (two separate project files, each with its own Tapir session). For each `DrawingItem` on a layout, I need to recreate an equivalent drawing (via `CreateDrawings`) linked to the corresponding source view, at the same position/scale/angle.

## What's missing

`CreateDrawings` lets me create a new Drawing with `position`, `scale`, etc. — but there's no command to **read** those values from an *existing* Drawing element.

I found that `ChangeDrawingLink` already reads exactly this data internally (`DocumentCreationCommands.cpp`, `ChangeDrawingLinkCommand::Execute`):

```cpp
API_Element oldElement = {};
oldElement.header.guid = oldGuid;
ACAPI_Element_Get (&oldElement);
...
newDrawingItem.Add ("position", Create2DCoordinateObjectState (oldElement.drawing.pos));
newDrawingItem.Add ("scale", oldElement.drawing.ratio);
newDrawingItem.Add ("angle", oldElement.drawing.angle);
newDrawingItem.Add ("drawingScale", oldElement.drawing.drawingScale);
newDrawingItem.Add ("modelOffset", Create2DCoordinateObjectState (oldElement.drawing.modelOffset));
```

It just never returns these fields to the caller — it only reuses them internally to recreate the drawing against a new link, within the same project.

## Request

Could a command (new, e.g. `GetDrawingSettings`, or an addition to an existing one) expose these same fields — `position`, `scale`, `angle`, `drawingScale`, `modelOffset`, plus the clip polygon — for a given Drawing `elementId`, read-only?

This would make it possible to script a faithful Layout Book replication/sync between two separate project files (not just within one project, which `ChangeDrawingLink` already covers).

Happy to test against a build if useful.

## Context

Same use case as #1422 / #1423 (syncing a Layout Book between two project files) — I need to delete obsolete layout subsets (groups) from the target project.

## What I found

In `NavigatorCommands.cpp`, `DeleteNavigatorItemsCommand::Execute`:

```cpp
if (navItem.itemType == API_LayoutNavItem || navItem.itemType == API_MasterLayoutNavItem) {
    API_DatabaseInfo dbInfo = navItem.db;
    err = ACAPI_Database_DeleteDatabase (&dbInfo);
} else if (navItem.itemType == API_SubSetNavItem) {
    err = APIERR_NOTSUPPORTED;
} else {
    ...
}
```

So deleting a `SubSetItem` (a Layout Book group/folder) always fails with "Deleting layout subsets is not supported by the ArchiCAD API."

## Question

Is this a deliberate limitation (the underlying `ACAPI_Navigator_*` call genuinely can't delete subsets), or a known gap that's just not implemented yet / could be revisited? I want to know whether to wait for this before scripting a "delete everything inside, then delete the empty subset folder" workflow, or whether that's never going to work and I should plan around it permanently (e.g. leave empty subset folders for manual deletion).

This continues #1202, whose part 1 (#1204, #1409) is merged, and takes over #1192–#1198. Parts 2–6 of #1202 and those seven issues stopped because the bot had no DevKit and asked for the declarations. Below is every function they still need, with its declaration in each Archicad version (from the AC25–AC30 DevKits the build checks use: 25.3002, 26.3000, 27.3001, 28.3001, 29.3000, 30.3000), and every struct and enum it uses. As the requester I've made the design decisions the bot asked for.

**For every part:**
- New commands go in the existing *Analytical Commands* group, next to `GetCurrentAnalyticalModel`. Call with `ACAPI_GetToken ()` as part 1 does.
