This builds the functions that the six parts of #1538 (#1539–#1544) left out only because the issue bot's runner has no DevKit. Everything the bot couldn't see is below, copied from the AC25–AC30 DevKits the build checks use, including Graphisoft's own example code (`Examples/LibPart_Test`, `3D_Test`, `IFC_Test`, `Environment_Control`). As the requester I've made the design decisions.

**For every part:**
- Branch from `main`. Don't wait for #1539–#1544 or any other open PR, and never change a command another open PR adds.
- Match the surrounding code and reuse the `CommandBase` helpers. Register each new command in `AddOnMain.cpp` with the version `main` carries, add a Python example, and gate version-specific calls with `ServerMainVers_*` and the `MigrationHelper.hpp` mappings for Archicad 25–26.
- Writes run in one `ACAPI_CallUndoableCommand` per call, except where a section says the call is non-undoable. One execution result per item.

Four parts. Parts 1–3 are independent; part 4 is the largest.

## 1. 3D: texture coordinates and convex polygons

**`GetTextureCoordinates {elements}`**: per element, per body, per polygon, the `(u, v)` texture coordinates at the polygon's first vertex. `API_TexCoordPars.elemIdx` and `bodyIdx` come from the body component, **minus one**, as `Examples/3D_Test/Src/3D_Test.c` does:

```cpp
// ACAPI_ModelAccess_Get3DInfo (elemHead, &info3D) gives info3D.fbody .. info3D.lbody
component.header.typeID = API_BodyID;
component.header.index  = ibody;                        // fbody .. lbody
ACAPI_ModelAccess_GetComponent (&component);
elemIdx = component.body.head.elemIndex - 1;
bodyIdx = component.body.head.bodyIndex - 1;            // if either is < 0: no texture info for this body
for (j = 1; j <= nPgon; j++) {                          // API_PgonID, index j within the body
    API_TexCoordPars pars = {0};
    pars.elemIdx = elemIdx;
    pars.bodyIdx = bodyIdx;
    pars.pgonIndex = j;
    // surface point: pgon.fpedg -> API_PedgID -> abs (pedg.pedg) -> API_EdgeID -> abs (edge.vert1) -> API_VertID
    pars.surfacePoint.x = component.vert.x;  /* .y, .z */
    err = ACAPI_ModelAccess_GetTextureCoord (&pars, &resultUV);   // API_UVCoord {double u, v;}
}
```
```cpp
GSErrCode ACAPI_ModelAccess_GetTextureCoord (API_TexCoordPars* texCoordPars, API_UVCoord* uvCoord);   // AC27+; AC25–26: ACAPI_Goodies (APIAny_GetTextureCoordID, &pars, &uv)
typedef struct {
  UInt32 elemIdx;
  UInt32 bodyIdx;
  UInt32 pgonIndex;
  UInt32 filler_1;
  API_Coord3D surfacePoint;
} API_TexCoordPars;
```

**`convex: true` on `TriangulatePolygonsOfElements`** (on `main`): after `ACAPI_ModelAccess_GetComponent` for the body (`API_BodyID`), which makes it the active body, call `ACAPI_ModelAccess_DecomposePgon (j, &cpoly)` for polygon `j` of that body. Return each convex sub-polygon as a list of vertex coordinates. The `cpoly` layout, from the DevKit remark:

```cpp
GSErrCode ACAPI_ModelAccess_DecomposePgon (Int32 ipgon, Int32 ***cpoly);   // AC27+; AC25–26: ACAPI_3D_DecomposePgon, same signature
// cpoly holds:  [-n], [-m1], i1 .. i(m1), [-m2], j1 .. j(m2), ...
//   n  = number of subpolygons (0 on error or for a degenerate polygon), stored negative
//   mi = number of vertices of the following subpolygon, stored negative
//   i1 .. are VERT indices of the body (no new points are created)
// APIERR_REFUSEDCMD: no active body. APIERR_BADINDEX: ipgon out of range.
// "Do not forget to dispose the returned handle": BMKillHandle (reinterpret_cast<GSHandle*> (&cpoly));
```

